  "metadata": {
    "title": "Standardized Framework and Quantitative Benchmarking: Nuclear Segmentation platforms for Multiplexed Immunofluorescence data in Translational Studies",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.13306714",
    "publication_date": "2024",
    "description": "<p>Paired nuclear ground truth annotated binary masks and segmentation binary masks predicted from different segmentation platforms can be found in <strong>external.zip</strong> and <strong>inhouse.zip</strong>. <strong>internal.zip</strong> was used for development of the nuclear segmentation evaluation pipeline while <strong>external.zip</strong> hosts data that was/can be used for benchmarking of nuclear segmentation performance for multiplexed immunofluorescence workflows in translational studies. <strong>external.zip</strong> contains ground-truth annotated binary masks from another dataset in synapse (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.7303/SYN27624812\">https://doi.org/10.7303/SYN27624812</a>). <strong>README_DAPI.md</strong> contains information about where to locate the raw DAPI nuclear intensities used by the different segmentation platforms to predict nuclei.</p>\n<p>The naming convention of the ROIs used is as follows: \"Sample ID_ULHC\" where ULHC refers to the coordinates of the upper-left hand corner pixel of the ROI within the whole sample field, delimited by an underscore (\"_\"). E.g. For ROI \"Bph2_45174_14597_160_246\": \"Bph2_45174_14597\" is the sample ID and (246,160) is the ULHC pixel.</p>\n<h2>&nbsp;</h2>\n<h2><strong>Citing</strong></h2>\n<p>Please cite these sources if you use this data</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sankaranarayanan, A., Khachaturov, G., Smythe, K.S. &amp; Mittal, S<em>.</em>&nbsp;Quantitative benchmarking of nuclear segmentation algorithms in multiplexed immunofluorescence imaging for translational studies.&nbsp;<em>Commun Biol</em>&nbsp;<strong>8</strong>, 836 (2025). <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-025-08184-8\">https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-025-08184-8</a></li>\n<li>Sankaranarayanan, A., Khachaturov, G., Smythe, K. S. &amp; Mittal, S. Standardized framework and quantitative benchmarking: nuclear segmentation platforms for multiplexed immunofluorescence data in translational studies.&nbsp;<em>Zenodo</em> (2024). <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.12775616\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.12775616</a></li>\n</ol>",
